cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use OpenRook in academic work, please cite it as below."
title: "OpenRook: A Cognitive Runtime for Persistent, Experience-Grounded Agents"
abstract: >-
  OpenRook is an experimental cognitive runtime that gives LLM agents
  persistent state outside the model: memory, identity, relational
  continuity, affective state, and accumulated learning. It treats
  emotion as persistent internal state rather than a stylistic prompt
  layer, combining six continuous modulators (arousal, valence,
  certainty, bonding, energy, resolution) with a multi-layer memory
  system (short-term, long-term, relationship arc, semantic memory,
  and a shared Life History store for identity-level experiences),
  unified self/other profiling, model-based appraisal with deterministic
  fallback, response-strategy selection, bounded dual-process deliberation,
  optional expression shaping in conversational mode, a self-evolution
  metabolism (belief decay, theme-to-belief promotion, drive-gap
  detection), trigger-time skill loading, and a gated agentic tool
  layer (off by default; filesystem sandboxed; shell as a separate
  opt-in). An opt-in research runtime adds persistent self-selected goals,
  evidence-verified actions, outcome learning, functional appraisal controls,
  preregistered paired studies, and recorded transition replay. Controlled-world
  retained-choice evidence is distinct from human equivalence or subjective
  emotion. The implementation is inspired by PSI Theory, ACT-R, and
  CLARION, but is presented as engineering-oriented synthesis rather
  than a faithful implementation of any one framework.
